From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 21:58:50 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] engine: deliver the swap chain handle in the SwapChainChanged
 args

The ghostty pane came up completely blank. libghostty was rendering
correctly the whole time - nothing was ever bound to the panel.

TermControl::RenderEngineSwapChainChanged takes the handle from the
event args, boxed as a uint64, and never re-reads SwapChainHandle():

    HANDLE h = reinterpret_cast<HANDLE>(winrt::unbox_value<uint64_t>(args));

We raised the event with null args, so the unbox threw instead of
binding. And that handler is the only place a normal (non-reattach)
startup ever calls _AttachDxgiSwapChainToXaml, so the panel never got a
swap chain at all - hence blank rather than merely late.

Now boxes the handle the way ControlCore does, re-reading it on the UI
thread so the newest one wins, and raises once more at the end of
Initialize in case the renderer built the swap chain during
ghostty_surface_new. That second raise is a no-op while the handle is 0.

Verified by capture: a WT tab titled "ghostty test" showing cmd.exe's
banner and prompt, rendered by libghostty through the D3D11 backend into
a DirectComposition swap chain on the XAML SwapChainPanel.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_012ee27Fih3vSPYsqrUBpUoc
---
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp    | 34 ++++++++++++++++---
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h      |  1 +
 2 files changed, 31 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
index 04a2cdfae..1933db2e9 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
@@ -69,6 +69,11 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         }
 
         _initializedTerminal = true;
+
+        // If the renderer already built a swap chain while we were in
+        // ghostty_surface_new, its action fired before anything could act on
+        // it. Raising once here costs nothing when the handle is still 0.
+        _raiseSwapChainChanged();
         return true;
     }
     catch (...)
@@ -341,10 +346,15 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
 
         case GHOSTTY_ACTION_SWAP_CHAIN_CHANGED:
         {
-            // Device loss produced a new handle; the panel's binding is stale.
-            // This is a UI-thread event in IControlCore, and getting it wrong
-            // shows as a frozen pane rather than an error.
-            _raiseOnUiThread([this]() { SwapChainChanged.raise(*this, nullptr); });
+            // The handle travels in the event args, boxed as a uint64 -
+            // TermControl::RenderEngineSwapChainChanged unboxes it and binds
+            // that, and never re-reads SwapChainHandle(). Raising this with
+            // null args is not a no-op, it throws in the unbox.
+            //
+            // This is also the *only* path that ever binds the panel on a
+            // normal (non-reattach) startup, which is why getting it wrong
+            // shows up as a permanently blank pane rather than a late one.
+            _raiseSwapChainChanged();
             return true;
         }
 
@@ -404,6 +414,22 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         return false;
     }
 
+    // Re-read the handle and hand it to the control, boxed the way
+    // TermControl expects. Read on the UI thread rather than captured at the
+    // raise site: the renderer may have replaced it again in between, and the
+    // last one wins either way.
+    void GhosttyControlCore::_raiseSwapChainChanged()
+    {
+        _raiseOnUiThread([this]() {
+            const auto handle = SwapChainHandle();
+            if (handle == 0)
+            {
+                return;
+            }
+            SwapChainChanged.raise(*this, winrt::box_value<uint64_t>(handle));
+        });
+    }
+
     // Events that IControlCore documents as UI-thread must arrive there. If we
     // are already on it, run inline so ordering with the caller is preserved.
     void GhosttyControlCore::_raiseOnUiThread(std::function<void()> action)
diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h
index 091d23313..955343863 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h
@@ -238,6 +238,7 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         void _applySettingsToConfig(ghostty_config_t config) const;
         void _connectionOutputHandler(const winrt::array_view<const char16_t>& data);
         void _connectionStateChangedHandler();
+        void _raiseSwapChainChanged();
         void _raiseOnUiThread(std::function<void()> action);
 
         Control::IControlSettings _settings{ nullptr };
